      "Several texts place Christ at the very start of God’s purpose. “He chose us in him before the foundation of the world” (Ephesians 1:4). Christ “was foreknown indeed before the foundation of the world, but was revealed in this last age for your sake” (1 Peter 1:20). “All things have been created through him, and for him” (Colossians 1:16). The Incarnation was not a scramble after something went wrong.",
      "Revelation 13:8 in the KJV speaks of “the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world”. This is a beautiful phrase, but honesty requires a note: the Greek word order is ambiguous, and many translations (for example the ESV and NRSV, compare Revelation 17:8) attach “from the foundation of the world” to the names written in the book of life instead. So the verse supports the idea, but not decisively.",
      "<strong>The Franciscan thesis.</strong> John Duns Scotus (c. 1266–1308), building on earlier thinkers such as Rupert of Deutz and Robert Grosseteste, argued that God willed the Incarnation for its own sake, as the highest work of his love, and that Christ’s glory could not depend on the accident of human sin. Christ would have come even if Adam had never fallen; sin changed the <em>manner</em> of his coming (suffering and death), not the fact. Many in the Eastern tradition read Maximus the Confessor (especially <em>Ad Thalassium</em> 60, which calls the mystery of Christ the blessed end for which all things were made) in a similar direction, though scholars debate how far Maximus explicitly addressed the question. Irenaeus also saw Adam as created toward Christ, the true image.",
      "<strong>Aquinas’ view.</strong> Thomas Aquinas (<em>Summa Theologiae</em> III, q.1, a.3) weighed the question and concluded that, since Scripture everywhere gives sin as the reason for the Incarnation, it is more fitting to say the Incarnation was ordained as a remedy for sin, so that without sin it would not have happened. But he added that God’s power is not limited by this: God could have become incarnate even without sin. Much Western theology has followed Aquinas, while many others have followed Scotus.",
      "<strong>Modern voices.</strong> Karl Barth (<em>Church Dogmatics</em> II/2) placed the election of Jesus Christ at the very beginning of God’s ways: God’s first decision was to be God-with-us in Jesus. Baxter Kruger, in <em>The Great Dance</em> and elsewhere, teaches that the Father, Son and Spirit always purposed to share their life with humanity through the Son becoming human; the Fall did not create the plan but was overcome within it. The relational gospel generally stands with this “Christ first” view, while recognising that the question is honestly debated and that good theologians have answered it differently."
